WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.760 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  in the early hours of September 1st 1939  the German battleship chalets Vikash  Stein attacked a Polish ammunition depot  in Danzig now known as Gdansk  they were the opening shots of world war  ii german bombers also destroyed via  loon but town and western poland of no  strategic importance 1200 civilians were  killed in the vomits first war crime  shortly afterwards the German army  crossed the Polish border a scene  re-enacted for these propaganda pictures  the previous evening SS troops wearing  Polish uniforms had staged an attack on  a German radio station Hitler used this  as a pretext to invade Poland German  troops also attacked the Polish post  office in Danzig the siege lasted  fifteen hours most of the defenders were  shot dead after a German court-martial  but Hitler's ambitions went far beyond  Poland he wanted to conquer Europe and  impose his racist Nazi ideology on the  entire continent the early weeks of the  conflict saw war crimes committed  against polish civilians Poland's allies  Britain and France declared war on  Germany but they were not prepared to  intervene militarily and there was no  help from Moscow the Soviet Union had  given Nazi Germany an almost free hand  Germany and the Soviets had publicly  agreed on a non-aggression pact and  secretly made a deal to carve up Eastern  Europe between the two dictatorships not  long after the German invasion of Poland  the Soviet Union moved in from the east  Poland's fate was sealed when German  forces and the Red Army met at the  agreed demarcation line well now there  are growing demands in Poland for  Germany to pay reparations worth several  billion euros as compensation for the  devastation  country cost the Topix likely to be  raised as Germany's delegation visits  Warsaw on Sunday for commemorations the  question of reparations has caused  tension between Germans and poles  Warsaw's old town a few days before the  80th anniversary of the start of the  Second World War  Leo Mouse Bach walked Sethe route every  day on his way home from work the 29  year old German has lived in Poland for  five years he actively supports German  Polish economic cooperation but he  didn't expect this particular debate  topic to resurface I was surprised when  I heard about Poland's demands for  German reparations and I was seriously  shocked when I saw that many of my  friends young poles who don't sympathize  with the government we're saying that  they could understand why the government  wants this why they feel the Germany has  not yet dealt sufficiently with what  they did  Warsaw's Old Town is a symbol of German  guilt the city was completely destroyed  during the Second World War and then  rebuild without any help from Germany  and now after almost 80 years Berlin is  being handed the bill a parliamentary  committee has been working on a draft  for the government so they can finally  put this topic to rest even though for  decades they have already considered it  closed doesn't like the fact we're  talking about reparations we understand  that but for us it is an important topic  at the moment we are still calculating  the exact reparations sums we want to  present this to Berlin very soon but  Berlin doesn't seem open to this  presentation so far they don't even want  to talk about it the topic is considered  legally closed the other markets in the  Polish government hasn't even sent us an  official message that if they do then  the legal situation requires that we  return to the treaties of 1953 and the  two plus four treaty of 1990 the law  considers these clamps as regulated and  therefore non-existent of the machine  in fact in 1953 communist Poland  renounced reparations from the then  communist East Germany so Berlin  suggested symbolic gestures instead here  for example in the middle of the city  there are plans for a memorial site for  Polish victims of the German occupation  during World War 2 to be built  Leo Massa the young German living in  Warsaw actively supports this idea he  believes that such political gestures  could make the upcoming historical  debates easier I think this would be a  very important sign of compassion it  would be a symbol of empathy for the  poles and a sign that we as Germans  should never forget what happened and  this is why Leo Mouse paw will be here  on Sunday when the presidents of Poland  and Germany remembered the 80th  anniversary of the German invasion

